{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1776880598,"nonce":"M2eVaV+oihZZ8l0QJpEUdyLqAPE=","title":"Support Codex OAuth authentication","message":"## Problem\n\nCurrent Codex setup only supports API key auth (`CODEX_API_KEY`). Users who have run `codex auth login` on host should be able to use those OAuth tokens in containers.\n\n## Solution\n\nMount `~/.codex` from host into container, similar to how we handle `~/.claude` for Claude Code auth.\n\n## Files to modify\n\n- `host/docker_cmd.py`:\n  - Add `-v ~/.codex:/codex-auth:ro` mount when Codex auth dir exists\n  \n- `docker-entrypoint.sh`:\n  - If `/codex-auth` exists, copy to `$HOME/.codex`\n  - Skip `config.toml` generation when OAuth tokens are present (check for token file)\n  \n- `Dockerfile`:\n  - Ensure codex auth directory is handled in user setup\n\n## Tests\n\n- `test_codex_oauth_mount_added` - docker_cmd adds codex auth mount when dir exists\n- `test_codex_oauth_skips_config_generation` - entrypoint skips config.toml when OAuth present\n- `test_codex_oauth_fallback_to_api_key` - falls back to API key when no OAuth\n\nREQ: REQ-031","files":null}]}